1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:09,575
IETF 127 Hackathon: CoAP over Bundle Protocol version 7, the Aggregate demonstration.

2
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:17,480
In this demo, two CoAP POST messages travel inside one BPv7 request bundle.

3
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:23,404
The Rover Science client combines the two Non-confirmable POSTs at the application layer.

4
00:00:23,454 --> 00:00:28,766
The Satellite only forwards bundles, and Earth hosts the CoAP application server.

5
00:00:28,816 --> 00:00:35,724
This is an edited walkthrough of real Rover and Earth screenshots from our test on 7 November.

6
00:00:37,124 --> 00:00:40,360
Here is Mission Control, set up for the experiment.

7
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:44,649
We select Aggregate mode, and use the Science POST application.

8
00:00:44,699 --> 00:00:51,057
For this controlled lab test, the GUI and the Rover engine both use NSTART = 2.

9
00:00:52,457 --> 00:00:56,993
Next, press Send POST for Rover Science twice, within ten seconds.

10
00:00:57,043 --> 00:01:03,915
The first click fills buffer slot one, and the second click fills slot two, each with its own message ID.

11
00:01:03,965 --> 00:01:08,405
Both requests now wait together in one Rover application buffer.

12
00:01:09,805 --> 00:01:12,335
Mission Control confirms the result.

13
00:01:12,385 --> 00:01:20,826
The Rover event log reports one Aggregate submission: two CoAP Singles, in a 556-byte bundle payload.

14
00:01:20,876 --> 00:01:27,711
Earth sends back two 2.01 Created replies, and each one is matched independently by its Token.

15
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:32,339
Now, the real Wireshark capture taken on Earth.

16
00:01:32,389 --> 00:01:39,514
Frame 36 is the request bundle, from ipn:1.11 to ipn:3.64.

17
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:48,911
It carries a 556-byte BPv7 payload, which our dissector classifies as a CoAP Aggregate with two messages.

18
00:01:50,311 --> 00:01:55,802
Expanding frame 36, the custom Lua dissector shows the Aggregate structure.

19
00:01:55,852 --> 00:02:01,648
There are two members, CoAP member #1 and CoAP member #2, and the message count is 2.

20
00:02:03,047 --> 00:02:07,712
Member #1 is a Non-confirmable POST, code 0.02.

21
00:02:07,762 --> 00:02:16,701
Its 24-bit message ID is 2543266, and it carries Token 80f16d65.

22
00:02:16,751 --> 00:02:20,043
The Uri-Path is science / observations.

23
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:26,750
And the Payload-length option, number 65000, gives 227 bytes for this member.

24
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:32,873
Member #2 is also a Non-confirmable POST, to the same resource.

25
00:02:32,923 --> 00:02:41,389
It has a different message ID, 2543267, and a different Token, 98b6b902.

26
00:02:41,439 --> 00:02:45,129
Its Payload-length is also 227 bytes.

27
00:02:46,529 --> 00:02:51,457
The BPv7 primary block shows the bundle's lifetime on the wire.

28
00:02:51,507 --> 00:02:56,763
Wireshark reports 90,000 milliseconds: 90 seconds.

29
00:02:56,813 --> 00:03:03,759
That is the NON lifetime of 100 seconds, minus the maximum aggregation delay of 10 seconds.

30
00:03:03,809 --> 00:03:07,171
And the primary-block CRC is reported as good.

31
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:12,610
Now the responses. Frame 53 comes back from Earth.

32
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:18,370
It is a Non-confirmable 2.01 Created response, with message ID 59.

33
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:23,697
Its Token, 80f16d65, matches the first POST.

34
00:03:25,097 --> 00:03:27,848
Frame 70 is the second response.

35
00:03:27,898 --> 00:03:33,266
It is also a Non-confirmable 2.01 Created, with message ID 60.

36
00:03:33,316 --> 00:03:38,520
Its Token, 98b6b902, matches the second POST.

37
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:45,470
The result: two POSTs, one BPv7 request bundle, and two responses.

38
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:54,890
The application layer aggregated two messages, the bundle payload was 556 bytes, and the Rover matched both Earth replies.

39
00:03:54,940 --> 00:04:01,221
This test used NSTART = 2, with an all-NON Aggregate lifetime of 90 seconds.

40
00:04:01,271 --> 00:04:07,935
Functional aggregation is verified. But this exchange alone does not establish congestion safety:

41
00:04:07,985 --> 00:04:12,211
that is our next study, under contact limits, outages and load.

42
00:04:13,661 --> 00:04:20,178
Thank you for watching. The code and the Wireshark dissector are in our project repository.
